WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:08.080
My hope for the future
is to realise my full potential,

00:00:08.160 --> 00:00:10.680
and understand
why I was put on this earth.

00:00:10.760 --> 00:00:13.320
Hopefully, by the time I leave this world,

00:00:13.400 --> 00:00:16.640
I will understand my true purpose in life.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:21.160
My name's Patrick Cabral and I'm a multimedia artist.

00:00:22.160 --> 00:00:25.440
Since childhood, I've always believed
in challenging myself.

00:00:25.520 --> 00:00:28.480
It's important to pursue new things,
even if they seem impossible.

00:00:28.560 --> 00:00:34.280
The first time I remember drawing was in Kindergarten.

00:00:34.360 --> 00:00:38.160
I chose to draw the Leyte Landing -

00:00:38.240 --> 00:00:40.640
it was super detailed.

00:00:40.720 --> 00:00:43.840
When my teacher saw it,
she praised me in front of the class.

00:00:44.600 --> 00:00:47.040
That sparked my passion for drawing.

00:00:48.040 --> 00:00:51.720
You don't have to have raw talent to create something beautiful;

00:00:52.320 --> 00:00:54.040
what artists need is support

00:00:54.120 --> 00:00:56.360
from others to realise their full potential.

00:00:57.320 --> 00:01:00.480
I grew up in a province where 
people weren't that interested in art.

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:03.200
We didn't have museums or galleries,

00:01:03.280 --> 00:01:05.480
so the only thing to fuel me

00:01:05.560 --> 00:01:07.760
was my desire to succeed.

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:13.560
After years of practice, I finally found my unique style.

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:17.800
Papercutting was a skill I already had,

00:01:17.880 --> 00:01:24.960
so I combined this with lettering and typography.

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:30.760
I set myself a challenge to create a design in paper using tiny detail.

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:36.320
I asked myself, "What's the thinnest line that I can create using paper?"

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:38.000
It wasn't easy,

00:01:38.080 --> 00:01:40.240
but it helped me establish

00:01:40.880 --> 00:01:42.840
the concept of my work.

00:01:44.120 --> 00:01:47.960
The first papercut animal I made was a wolf.

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:51.114
It didn't involve much planning -I just wanted it to look nice!

00:01:53.480 --> 00:01:56.160
Now that I'm a practising designer,

00:01:56.240 --> 00:02:00.080
I aim for 50% concept, 50% execution.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:03.240
Planning is important,
as it's where ideas grow.

00:02:03.320 --> 00:02:06.480
It's through planning
that I wanted to show...

00:02:10.040 --> 00:02:16.080
...parallels between my papercut animals
and our endangered species.

00:02:16.160 --> 00:02:17.480
Both are fragile.

00:02:19.720 --> 00:02:23.840
There's a quote I love:
"If you like a flower, you pluck it...

00:02:25.360 --> 00:02:29.960
...if you love a flower,
you water it every day."

00:02:30.040 --> 00:02:32.680
The same can be said for my craft.

00:02:32.760 --> 00:02:36.200
Being an artist requires time,
patience, effort and daily practice.

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:39.400
Ideas are always forming - my work

00:02:39.480 --> 00:02:42.000
will never cease to evolve.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:45.280
At the moment,I'm known for my paper artwork,

00:02:45.360 --> 00:02:48.840
but I want to develop and explore 
the possibilities of craft in other ways.

00:02:48.920 --> 00:02:51.520
Regardless of the medium, 
I'll always approach my work

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.720
with the same level of tenacity and precision

00:02:56.800 --> 00:03:00.000
that I've achieved in my paper art.

